            <ArticleTitle>Cushing's disease presenting with pituitary apoplexy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1586-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jocn.2011.10.017</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0967-5868(12)00032-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pituitary tumour apoplexy is a rare but life threatening condition. Cushing's disease usually presents with clinical features of Cushing's syndrome. We report a 30-year-old male patient with Cushing's disease who presented with severe headache and right third nerve palsy. MRI of the pituitary gland revealed a pituitary adenoma with infarction suggestive of apoplexy. After a transsphenoidal surgery he developed pan-hypopituitarism with diabetes insipidus. We also review the relevant literature.</AbstractText>
